  "response": "# Swallowed-error review: four findings, two of them hide the failure\n\nI read the four catch blocks and their callers in `04-monorepo`. One point first: three of the four findings say the error is lost because \"the log call omits the error value\". That part is wrong. Each of those handlers calls `handleError(error)` before `logger.error`, and `handleError` (`src/errors.ts:16-38`) prints `error.stack`, sets `process.exitCode = 1`, and posts `'error'` to the parent when running in a worker. So the stack does get printed.\n\nThe real problem is control flow. The catch blocks don't rethrow, so the function returns normally and anything awaiting it carries on as if the build worked.\n\n## 1. `src/tsc.ts:215` — `runTypeScriptCompiler`: **hides the failure**\n\n- Evidence: `\"what happens to the failure: the handler logs a message but never passes the error, so the stack, cause chain, and error code are lost\"`, `\"missing signal: no rethrow or rejected promise; … no typed error result the caller could branch on\"`\n- This function is supposed to return `exports`. When the catch runs, it returns `undefined` instead. The caller at `src/index.ts:199-200` passes that straight on with `await runDtsRollup(options, exports)`.\n- The real tsc error is turned into a missing value, which then fails one step later with a misleading message. Not recoverable: without declarations there's nothing to roll up.\n\n## 2. `src/api-extractor.ts:197` — `runDtsRollup`: **hides the failure (fix this first)**\n\n- Evidence: `\"protected operation: const start = Date.now() (+6 more statement(s))\"`, `\"calls inside the protected region: Date.now, Math.floor, logger.info, cleanDtsFiles, rollupDtsFiles, logger.success\"`, `\"missing signal: no rethrow or rejected promise; a log call is present but the error value is not passed to it; no typed error result the caller could branch on; the error is never inspected\"`\n- This catch handles both its own failures and the knock-on failure from #1, since the block contains `throw new Error('Unexpected internal error: dts exports is not define')`.\n- After it swallows the error, `runDtsRollup` resolves, so `experimentalDtsTask` and then `dtsTask` resolve too. `Promise.all([dtsTask(), mainTasks()])` at `src/index.ts:455` succeeds, `build()` resolves, and `mainTasks` goes on to `copyPublicDir` and the user's `onSuccess` hook (lines 349-352) even though no declaration files were produced.\n- The only remaining failure signal is `process.exitCode = 1`, and two things defeat it:\n  - Anyone calling `build()` from code sees a resolved promise.\n  - `src/run.ts:8` sets `process.exitCode = code || 0`, which can overwrite it with 0.\n\n  That matches \"CI green on a broken release\".\n\n## 3. `src/rollup.ts:187` — `runRollup`: **failure does get out, but only by a fragile path**\n\n- Evidence: `\"calls inside the protected region: Date.now, Math.floor, logger.info, rollup, Promise.all, options.outputConfig.map\"`, `\"what happens to the failure: the handler logs a message but never passes the error, so the stack, cause chain, and error code are lost\"`\n- This runs in a worker. `handleError` posts `'error'`, and the parent rejects on that message (`src/index.ts:241-243`). Then `runRollup` returns normally, so `startRollup` (line 222-223) also posts `'success'`. Because `'error'` arrives first, the failure happens to get through.\n- Not recoverable. It works today only because of a side channel inside `handleError`. It should rethrow so the existing `catch { parentPort?.postMessage('error') }` in `startRollup` is what actually reports it.\n\n## 4. `src/utils.ts:135` — `jsoncParse`: **mostly recoverable, but worth noting**\n\n- Evidence: `\"protected operation: return new Function(`return ${strip(data).trim()}`)()\"`, `\"what happens to the failure: the handler returns `{}` without inspecting the error\"`, `\"the catch declares no binding, so the error object is not even available to the handler\"`\n- The code says this is deliberate: `// That's what tsc/jsonc-parser did after all`. That's a fair default when reading tsconfig files you don't own.\n- But it also breaks a caller. `loadJson` in `src/load.ts:10-24` wraps the call in a try/catch meant to produce `Failed to parse <file>`. That error can never fire, because `jsoncParse` never throws. A malformed JSON config file quietly loads as `{}`, so tsup builds with default settings. It's low priority, but splitting it into a strict version for config loading and a lenient one for tsconfig would be right.\n\n## The fix to make first: `runDtsRollup` (`src/api-extractor.ts:197`)\n\nThis one comes first because it is the last catch on the experimental-dts path before `build()`. Rethrowing here makes failures from both tsc (#1) and api-extractor reject `build()`, and stops `onSuccess` from running after a broken build.\n\n```ts\n  } catch (error) {\n    logger.error('dts', 'Build error')\n    throw error\n  }\n```\n\n`handleError` is dropped here on purpose. The CLI entry points already do `main().catch(handleError)` (`src/cli-default.ts:5`, `src/cli-node.ts:7`), so keeping it would print the stack twice.\n\nMake the same change in `runTypeScriptCompiler` (#1) right after, so the error that surfaces is the real tsc one and not \"dts exports is not define\". Then do the same in `runRollup` (#3).\n\nI made no edits and ran no builds or tests. The control-flow conclusions come from reading the code.",
